
-> What is Spring Boot?

 Spring Boot is a framework built on top of Spring that simplifies Spring application development.
   It helps you:
   Start applications quickly
   Avoid heavy configuration
   Focus on business logic, not setup

-> Why Spring Boot was introduced?
Traditional Spring applications had:
    Too much XML
    Manual dependency management
    External server setup
    Long project setup time


-> Limitations of Traditional Spring Framework
    | Problem               | Traditional Spring       |
    | --------------------- | ------------------------ |
    | Configuration         | Large XML files          |
    | Dependency Management | Manual version matching  |
    | Server                | External Tomcat required |
    | Startup Time          | Slow                     |
    | Boilerplate Code      | Very high                |

-> Spring Framework vs Spring Boot
    | Feature          | Spring Framework | Spring Boot   |
    | ---------------- | ---------------- | ------------- |
    | Configuration    | XML + Java       | Auto-config   |
    | Setup Time       | Long             | Very fast     |
    | Server           | External         | Embedded      |
    | Dependencies     | Manual           | Starter-based |
    | Production Ready | Extra setup      | Built-in      |

-> Role of @SpringBootApplication
    @SpringBootApplication
    public class DemoApplication {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
        }
    }

-> What does it do?
   It is a combination of 3 annotations:
    @SpringBootConfiguration
    @EnableAutoConfiguration
    @ComponentScan

-> What is Auto-Configuration?

    Auto-configuration means:

    Spring Boot automatically configures beans based on:
    Classpath dependencies
    Existing beans
    Properties

    Example:
    If spring-boot-starter-web is present
    → DispatcherServlet + Tomcat auto-configured

-> How Auto-Configuration Works Internally (Simple)
    Application starts
    Spring checks @EnableAutoConfiguration
    Reads spring.factories
    Matches dependencies
    Creates beans automatically

-> Starter Dependencies & Their Purpose
    What are Starters?
    Starters are dependency bundles.
    Instead of adding 10 dependencies manually, you add 1 starter.

-> Commonly Used Starters
    | Starter                        | Use             |
    | ------------------------------ | --------------- |
    | `spring-boot-starter-web`      | REST & MVC apps |
    | `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` | Database + ORM  |
    | `spring-boot-starter-security` | Security        |


